<section class="section">
  <div class="wrap-text center">
    <p class="eyebrow">Research</p>
    <h1>How, where, and why diversity assembles</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      We connect microevolutionary processes and species interactions to the
      generation, assembly, and maintenance of species diversity, empirically at
      the local community level and comparatively at the biogeographic and
      macroevolutionary level.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--band">
  <div class="wrap-text">
    <h2>Introduced species as natural experiments</h2>
    <p>
      A theme running under all three of the programmes below is our use of
      introduced species. Introductions are experiments in ecology and evolution
      at temporal and spatial scales no one could establish deliberately: novel
      abiotic conditions, novel species interactions, and communities assembling
      from scratch, all with a known start date. South Florida, where a diverse
      community of tropical lizards has arrived over the past century, is
      probably the best place in the world to take advantage of this.
    </p>
    <p>
      They let us ask questions that are otherwise almost unaskable. How do
      species respond to novel abiotic environments? What are the outcomes of
      novel species interactions? How do communities assemble when every
      interaction is new? Do niches expand, contract, or stay stable during an
      introduction?
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Theme I</p>
        <h2>Measuring natural selection in the wild</h2>
        <p>
          Our long-term study on Lizard Island asks how natural selection actually
          operates across individuals, species, space, and time, not in theory but
          as a measurable process in a real community. The community has five
          species: two semi-terrestrial specialists, two arboreal canopy
          specialists, and one large trunk specialist. We sample all of them,
          between 800 and 1,000 lizards at any given time, every spring and every
          fall.
        </p>
        <p>
          That schedule is the point. Sampling twice a year lets us measure
          selection through the summer reproductive season and separately through
          the winter non-reproductive season, resolving variation that an annual
          census would average away. The study began in Fall 2015 and has run
          since, with a forced hiatus during COVID.
        </p>
        <p>
          Ambition for the project is explicit: we are modelling it on the Grants'
          long-term study of Darwin's finches in the Galápagos, and we intend it to
          become one of the best known evolutionary field studies in the world.
        </p>
      </div>
      <figure>
        <img src="/assets/img/photos/sagrei-dewlap-rock.jpg" alt="A brown anole extending its orange dewlap">
        <figcaption>
          A brown anole extends its dewlap. Signal size scales with body size across
          anoles, a relationship we described in <em>Biology Letters</em> in 2023.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--band">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="split">
      <figure>
        <img src="/assets/img/photos/carolinensis-portrait.jpg" alt="A green anole">
        <figcaption>
          <em>Anolis carolinensis</em>, which colonised south Florida from Cuba
          roughly 5 to 7 million years ago and now ranges to North Carolina.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Theme II</p>
        <h2>The evolution of thermal ecophysiology</h2>
        <p>
          How do ecology and behavior shape the evolution of thermal physiology?
          We approach this from two directions.
        </p>
        <p>
          The first is a gradient. Green anoles reached south Florida from tropical
          Cuba about 5 to 7 million years ago and now live from the subtropics to
          temperate Tennessee and North Carolina. Our initial work suggests the
          physiological ceilings are not what changed: lower thermal limits differ
          surprisingly little across that range, especially set against how much
          the cold itself varies. Behavior is what differs. Where temperatures drop
          below their critical limit, green anoles stop being obligate arboreal
          specialists and move into subterranean thermal refugia, a shift we are
          tracking by walking the same transects weekly at night and recording the
          air temperature at which the lizards disappear from the trees.
        </p>
        <p>
          The second is speed. How fast can any of this happen? The tropical
          lizards that have arrived in Florida over the past century give us
          contemporary introductions with known start dates, which is what makes it
          possible to separate acclimation from adaptation. We combine ecological,
          physiological, and genomic and transcriptomic approaches, working toward
          an integrated account of why a species lives in one place and not
          another.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Theme III</p>
        <h2>New technology for eco-evolutionary field studies</h2>
        <p>
          Our third theme takes advantage of where the lab sits. Georgia Tech is one
          of the best engineering and computer science universities in the world,
          and integrating those tools into eco-evolutionary field work is the
          current frontier of the discipline.
        </p>
        <p>
          Ultra-lightweight wearable sensors let us track movement, behavior,
          physiology, and interactions passively, on free-living animals, in ways
          that were not previously achievable. Terrestrial lidar scans of Lizard
          Island are letting us identify and measure individual branches: perch
          diameter and perch height are the two axes anole ecomorphs are built
          around, and until now they have been measured by hand, one branch at a
          time. And we have written our own software for automated landmarking of
          our lizard radiographs and toepad scans, turning a bottleneck of
          human clicking into a batch process.
        </p>
        <p><a href="/software/">See the software and tools</a></p>
      </div>
      <figure>
        <img src="/assets/img/photos/carolinensis-specimen.jpg" alt="A green anole photographed against a white background">
        <figcaption>
          Automated landmarking turns images like this into morphometric data
          without a person placing every point.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

